)-f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x)}{(g(x))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(\\frac{7}{2})=\\frac{{f}^{′}(\\frac{7}{2})\\cdot g(\\frac{7}{2})-f(\\frac{7}{2})\\cdot {g}^{′}(\\frac{7}{2})}{{(g(\\frac{7}{2}))}^{2}}\n$$\n\nFrom the graph above, we obtain that $f(\\frac{7}{2})=g(\\frac{7}{2})=1.$\n\nAdditionally:\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (3,4)$ equals $-2.$ So, ${f}^{′}(\\frac{7}{2})=-2.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (2,4)$ equals $0.$ So, ${g}^{′}(\\frac{7}{2})=0.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(\\frac{7}{2}) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(\\frac{7}{2})g(\\frac{7}{2})-f(\\frac{7}{2}){g}^{′}(\\frac{7}{2})}{{(g(\\frac{7}{2}))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-2)\\cdot (1)-1\\cdot 0}{{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097344 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186095988 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097344 #edb/ref 1069 "q-73072" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097344 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097344 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/68/68379dee045e8b5d4f123f91c0f8c953d2cbbe6475c5ca13d4435889343c625d.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{′}(-1.5)$ where $h(x)=f(x)g(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097344 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bba9ad1a-f133-594e-8f67-d3021de105b3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097344 #edb/ref 1074 "The product rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)={f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)+f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(-1.5)={f}^{′}(-1.5)\\cdot g(-1.5)+f(-1.5)\\cdot {g}^{′}(-1.5).\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs above, we obtain that $f(-1.5)=-0.5$ and $g(-1.5)=-0.5.$\n\nAdditionally:\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (-2.5,0)$ equals $1.$ So, ${f}^{′}(-1.5)=1.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (-3,-0.5)$ equals $0.$ So, ${g}^{′}(-1.5)=0.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(-1.5) & ={f}^{′}(-1.5)\\cdot g(-1.5)+f(-1.5)\\cdot {g}^{′}(-1.5) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-0.5)+(-0.5)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097726 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097727 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097726 #edb/ref 1061 41291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097726 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e61a8095-664c-5074-bafd-b31cdbdc3f9d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097726 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186097906 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097727 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097728 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097727 #edb/ref 1061 41292 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097727 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "60e6454d-edc8-5611-b706-381666da1147" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097727 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186096804 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097728 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097729 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097728 #edb/ref 1061 41295 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097728 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f318e6e8-6284-53bd-8a92-ea63b929f668" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097728 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186096805 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097729 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097730 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097729 #edb/ref 1061 41298 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097729 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "66cada44-df2e-5018-8802-42f12b055e2d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097729 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186096806 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097730 #edb/ref 1061 41301 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097730 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9a4e38fe-2c63-5d93-80db-eb1b12107085" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097730 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186096807 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097906 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the graph of the function $y = f(x)$ shown below. \n\n![](images/5b/5b7ca737255a0af8a8ea0412d74a5ef63d04fe5a6b441eae103e98d3c00bad1a.png)\n\nLet's use our knowledge of derivatives to find $f'(0),$ the derivative of $f$ at $x=0.$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $f'(0)$ represents the slope of the tangent to $y=f(x)$ at the point $x=0.$\n\n- The function $y = f(x)$ is made up of line segments.\n\n- So, to find $f'(0),$ we simply need to find the slope of $y = f(x)$ over an interval that contains $x=0,$ and where $f$ is represented by a single line segment.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider the graph of $y=f(x)$ over $x\\in [-2,1].$ \n\n![](images/80/80dd0f95c4bd8030bae693e17185d4becee29c7681d134d3ee50a3621a184b94.png)\n\nThis interval contains $x=0,$ and $f$ is represented by a single line segment over this interval.\n\nThe slope of $y=f(x)$ over this interval is equal to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δx} & =\\frac{2-1}{1-(-2)}=\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f'(0)=\\dfrac{1}{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097906 #edb/ref 1065 6803 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097906 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a67ce8b0-4e17-57ad-9f00-3f594bfe2ae3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097906 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238348 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{3}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238348 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238348 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "21c62e13-678a-598d-b191-e515187394a4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238457 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{1}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238457 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238457 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2da76ba1-bd8d-5ac3-9e2e-00feea2bfdaf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238518 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238518 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238518 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "35622553-ec3a-571d-9154-58bd121230ba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238735 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{3}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238735 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238735 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4f3f188f-d5ab-5d97-918c-dce468688130" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238848 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238848 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/