$\n\\frac{Δg}{Δx}=\\frac{1-4}{3-(-1)}=-\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${g}^{′}(2)=-\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(2) & ={g}^{′}(2)-{f}^{′}(2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240942 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240502 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240942 #edb/ref 1069 "q-104803" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240942 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240942 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/ec/ec9faab92da8de318d78e548adc35e8df20083816f8bafdacf0cfbbd497e084c.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{′}(-2)$ where $h(x)=f(x)g(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240942 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "720b1d63-4a80-5bc1-95a2-9593bdc22709" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240942 #edb/ref 1074 "The product rule and the addition rule for derivatives give\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)={f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)+f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(-2)={f}^{′}(-2)\\cdot g(-2)+f(-2)\\cdot {g}^{′}(-2).\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs above, we obtain that $f(-2)=0$ and $g(-2)=3.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (-4,0)$ equals $-1.$ So, ${f}^{′}(-2)=-1.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (-4,0)$ equals $\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, ${g}^{′}(-2)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(-2) & ={f}^{′}(-2)\\cdot g(-2)+f(-2)\\cdot {g}^{′}(-2)-{f}^{′}(-2) \\\\  & =-1\\cdot 3+0\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241071 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240622 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241071 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105497" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241071 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241071 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/08/089281519cd8926eb187c67d465666838b8143b4dd86e081ef3c151613fd27fd.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is given above. Find ${f}^{′}(4).$